WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
- Thank you for coming out
on your Friday afternoon

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.390
to learn more about RockEDU LAB Jumpstart.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.610
And so my name is Odaelys Pollard.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:08.865
I am the scientist-educator here

00:00:08.865 --> 00:00:11.250
at RockEDU Science Outreach.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.140
And I facilitate and manage
the LAB Jumpstart program

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.890
alongside some other
programming that we have

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.560
that engage with teachers,
scientists, and students.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.640
With us on the webinar,
we have Lizzie as well,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.763
who is our Program Coordinator.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.460
And we also have a couple
other members of our team

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.540
who might join us later on.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.030
And so our LAB Jumpstart program

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.417
has been around for a few years.

00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:39.360
And we know because of
the current pandemic,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.403
it may look a little different this year

00:00:41.403 --> 00:00:43.040
than it did in past year.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.160
So wanna give you all an overview

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.737
of what LAB Jumpstart is all about,

00:00:46.737 --> 00:00:49.160
what are we looking forward to doing,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.350
and what we expect from us, our students,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.863
and the program on a whole.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.660
So what is LAB Jumpstart?

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.239
So if you're not familiar,

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:05.220
the acronym LAB is actually
Learning at the Bench.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.570
And LAB Jumpstart, we started about four,

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.260
about five years, five, six years ago now.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.576
And so it's a combined after school

00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:16.154
and summer science research
experience for students

00:01:16.154 --> 00:01:18.091
who have not had a previous opportunity

00:01:18.091 --> 00:01:21.160
to get their hands on to science research,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.334
and who may have been interested,

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:26.310
but haven't been giving those leeway

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.510
to do things that they're interested

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.750
in terms of STEM and science.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.750
And so what this program does,
it takes place after school,

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.280
this is all pre-pandemic,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.050
and we met on Tuesdays and
Thursdays from 4:30 to 7,

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.560
and this would occur from March to June.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.640
So there are two
components of the program.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.810
There's a spring part and
then there's a summer part,

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.300
and the summer program starts in June,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.350
so that's June to August.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.530
And this year, we're thinking
about 20 hours per week,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.430
and I'll talk a little bit
more as to what that entail.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.570
For students who are
interested in LAB Jumpstart,

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.830
you have to commit to the full program.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.120
So it is a large commitment.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.170
And so we wanna make
sure that the students

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.030
that are joining us are
very much into doing so,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:16.041
are very interested, are super engaged,

00:02:16.041 --> 00:02:18.353
and are passionate about learning more.

00:02:27.015 --> 00:02:28.190
All right, so what are our goals?

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.420
So one of the goals for LAB Jumpstart

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.230
is we wanna provide high school students

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.610
a positive, successful science experience

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.920
under the guidance of supportive
and compassionate mentors.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.080
And so that would be RockEDU team,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.300
we also have a number of
advocates that will be involved,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.430
and I'll talk a little bit
more as to what that is,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.056
and we also have two
facilitators of the program

00:02:50.056 --> 00:02:52.711
who are graduate students who we mentor

00:02:52.711 --> 00:02:54.170
throughout this process.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.429
So we wanna make sure that
the students that we bring in

00:02:56.429 --> 00:02:59.620
feel included or they
feel that they belong

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.590
in order for us to give
them the positive experience

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.053
that they may not have
otherwise seen in STEM.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.576
We also wanna help high school students

00:03:09.576 --> 00:03:13.110
to gain professional
skills for their future

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.105
in terms of academic and career pursuits.

00:03:15.105 --> 00:03:16.936
So one thing we always like to say

00:03:16.936 --> 00:03:20.910
is that a lot of times, students
may be interested in STEM

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.710
or have a passion for STEM.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:23.570
They're not quite sure,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.640
but it's something about
STEM that gets them going.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.020
And so we're not really
interested in making sure

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.100
that these students go into a STEM career,

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.737
even though that would be amazing,

00:03:32.737 --> 00:03:35.300
but we wanna make sure
that they're equipped

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.500
for whatever their
future academic or career

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.650
looks like in the future.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.090
So we wanna make sure
that they're ready to go

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.271
for college applications,

00:03:44.271 --> 00:03:48.680
that they feel secure
when they enter college.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.700
And they feel secure going into any field

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.750
that they feel like they
wanna enter outside of STEM.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.473
Hopefully it is in STEM.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.880
So like I mentioned, our Jumpstart 2021

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.400
is actually going to be virtual.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.820
So we've always had it in our laboratory.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.410
The students would come in
to Rockefeller University

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.460
Tuesdays and Thursdays after school,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.740
they'll be here from 4:30 to 7,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.774
and they'll have dinner with us,

00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:18.892
we'll take them into the lab,

00:04:18.892 --> 00:04:21.159
we'll teach them all these great things.

00:04:21.159 --> 00:04:25.700
It was very much an engaging
and hands on experience.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.880
In March, the pandemic is still happening,

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.955
but it started in the New York area,

00:04:31.955 --> 00:04:33.975
the United States worldwide.

00:04:33.975 --> 00:04:38.716
And so we had to quickly revamp
our programming to virtual.

00:04:38.716 --> 00:04:41.700
And so last year was, this year
was actually the first time

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.430
we've gone through the virtual
process of LAB Jumpstart.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.790
And because everyone is unsure

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:47.020
of what the future may look like,

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.170
we're actually gonna go ahead

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.370
with doing LAB Jumpstart virtual for 2021.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.350
So what would that look like?

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.940
And so these sessions
will still take place

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.779
after school during the spring.

00:04:57.779 --> 00:05:00.989
They will all be through Zoom meetings.

00:05:00.989 --> 00:05:03.700
Most of the courses will be synchronous.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.820
So we'll be meeting together
on Tuesdays and Thursdays

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.400
from four to six.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.565
There may be some classes

00:05:08.565 --> 00:05:10.403
where you can do asynchronous work,

00:05:10.403 --> 00:05:11.897
but we'll give you the time

00:05:11.897 --> 00:05:14.690
outside of the Tuesdays
and Thursdays to do so.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:16.655
So we might meet for a shorter time

00:05:16.655 --> 00:05:19.102
on Tuesdays and Thursday
with expectations of you

00:05:19.102 --> 00:05:23.200
fulfilling what you need to
do in that hour timeframe.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.610
The spring session will be
from March 2nd to June 17th,

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.607
so the majority of the spring.

00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:32.111
And this year, we have
two graduate students,

00:05:32.111 --> 00:05:36.050
they're both from the
Laboratory of Social Evolution,

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.820
and actually do a lot of work with ants,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:39.470
which is really fascinating.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.513
And so they really look
at how evolutionary things

00:05:43.871 --> 00:05:46.103
work in terms of social,

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.800
in terms of our social interactions,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.670
and we can actually figure out
a lot of how that navigated

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.420
from ants even to human,

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.270
so how they work together,
how they're able to collab.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:03.740
And these two graduate
students are really engaged

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.030
with the student body,
they're really engaged,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.440
and they're very much interested

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.530
in teaching and mentoring students.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.820
And so these sessions
will consist of lectures,

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.414
discussions, workshops, we'll have panels,

00:06:16.414 --> 00:06:19.510
and we're also gonna have hands on work.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.450
So what we've tried this year is that

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.920
and what we will be
doing this year is that

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.020
we're gonna be sending kits to students,

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.039
and these will be mailed to you.

00:06:27.039 --> 00:06:28.720
All of these are approved

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.500
by our Environmental Health
and Safety Department

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.333
here at Rockefeller.

00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:36.740
So everything that we send to
you will be able to be used

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.609
in your homes or whatever the
environment is that you're in.

00:06:40.609 --> 00:06:44.140
Students will also be paired
with a scientist advocate.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.380
And so what that is, is a scientist here

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.990
at Rockefeller University,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.321
who will be there to advocate for you

00:06:51.321 --> 00:06:52.810
outside of the program.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.260
So they're there to provide
guidance and mentorship

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.615
in terms of things like communication,

00:06:57.615 --> 00:06:59.908
if you have presentations to do,

00:06:59.908 --> 00:07:02.720
helping you write your college essay,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.660
fine tuning your resume,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.940
any career advice that you're looking for.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.910
So we're really hoping
that these scientists

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.494
will be there as an additional support

00:07:10.494 --> 00:07:12.730
for you outside of the RockEDU team

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:14.973
and outside of the core facilitators.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.080
So I just wanna briefly introduce

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.980
our two LAB Jumpstart
facilitators for 2021.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.586
So on the left, we have Taylor Hart,

00:07:24.586 --> 00:07:26.847
and Taylor studies in neurobiology

00:07:26.847 --> 00:07:29.740
of how ants perceive pheromones,

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.977
and is working on developing tools

00:07:31.977 --> 00:07:35.930
for studying these different
societies that ants have.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.580
And Lindsey Lopes will be joining us

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.163
for the first time in 2021.

00:07:40.163 --> 00:07:43.525
And Lindsey studies how ants
communicate with each other

00:07:43.525 --> 00:07:45.353
by using chemical signals,

00:07:45.353 --> 00:07:48.510
and how the ant brain is able
to process these chemicals

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.386
and coordinate with their behavior.

00:07:50.386 --> 00:07:54.420
And so Taylor is actually
been with us this year.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.571
So it would be her first time

00:07:56.571 --> 00:07:59.260
or her second time
joining us for next year.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:04.260
So she's what we call our,
I guess, senior facilitator,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.470
and Lindsey will be joining us
for the first time this year.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.350
But they both work in the same laboratory,

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.330
they both work really well together.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.740
So I'm looking forward to an amazing year

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.350
with the two facilitators.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:18.010
And they just love mentoring,

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:19.610
they have a passion for teaching,

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.523
so I'm really excited about that.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.830
And so I just want to share some quotes

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:27.360
and I know a lot of people are hesitant

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.450
with everything virtual.

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:32.540
We're going through like Zoom
fatigue and pandemic fatigue.

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:33.870
And we really want to get out there,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:34.970
but we really wanna make sure

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.480
that all of our students are safe.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.160
And so these quotes are coming

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.370
from LAB Jumpstart students this year,

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:43.860
who did have to transition to virtual.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.347
And so one of their feedback was that,

00:08:46.347 --> 00:08:48.461
"RockEDU was good at getting
me out of my comfort zone

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:50.357
when speaking to new people.

00:08:50.357 --> 00:08:53.050
Through LAB Jumpstart and SSRP virtual,"

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.637
which is our summer
portion of the program,

00:08:55.637 --> 00:08:57.226
"I have learned to speak more openly."

00:08:57.226 --> 00:08:58.570
So through LAB Jumpstart,

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:00.990
we really trying to get
students' confidence

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.730
to a level where they feel
comfortable speaking to people,

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.745
they feel comfortable doing presentations.

00:09:06.745 --> 00:09:11.745
We work a lot with
doing oral presentations

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.120
or even learning how to
read scientific articles,

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.793
and how to present that
information to others.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.727
Another quote from another student is,

00:09:20.727 --> 00:09:23.210
"The Jumpstart program was
exactly the kind of science

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:24.480
that I enjoyed doing."

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.658
And I'm really hoping
that we can facilitate

00:09:26.658 --> 00:09:30.960
the same type of thing
with our students in 2021,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.536
where we bring the science to you

00:09:32.536 --> 00:09:37.536
in a way that's humanizing and
trying to link both science

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:43.050
and human and the process
and how that all entails

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.730
to make the world go round.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.705
And so I'm really hoping
that students this year

00:09:46.705 --> 00:09:49.553
find it just as fascinating
as students last year did.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.210
So in terms of the summer session,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.510
so the spring, like I mentioned,

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:59.690
would go from March to June

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:01.992
and so that would be the two hours

00:10:01.992 --> 00:10:03.918
on every Tuesdays and Thursdays.

00:10:03.918 --> 00:10:08.300
And these hours are
outside of the holidays,

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:11.260
and the days off that the DOE has.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:12.748
So we're working around the DOE calendar.

00:10:12.748 --> 00:10:15.300
So we're not meeting during spring break,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:16.940
and we're not meeting on holidays.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:20.110
Now the summer program is gonna run

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.177
from June 28 to August 12,

00:10:22.177 --> 00:10:25.510
with the exception of July 4th holiday.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.487
And so you're expected to
work about 20 hours a week,

00:10:28.487 --> 00:10:31.280
and this is both synchronous
and asynchronous work,

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.210
and you'll be doing
something along the lines

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.850
of what the scientific process looks like.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.900
And so we'll be giving, sending kits

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:42.740
that you can do science in your spaces.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:44.650
And so we're asking all students

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:46.263
who are involved in the spring program,

00:10:46.263 --> 00:10:48.970
to also participate in the summer session,

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.900
where you'll take part in core sessions,

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:53.270
where we are able to
contextualize the relationship

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.330
between science and society.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.890
We're hoping to promote critical thinking.

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:01.290
All of the students will be
placed in small group tracks.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:02.700
And so within each track,

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.139
we actually have a team
of mentors and scientists

00:11:05.139 --> 00:11:07.680
that are there to work with elements

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.040
of your research project.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.180
So they're the ones that
kinda heading your project,

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:15.500
but you're kind of like the the brain

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:20.090
around developing this
project into something that

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:24.630
and looks great is relative
to different people.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:27.970
And so we also host a variety of events

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:31.610
to promote networking, we
engage in relevant discussions,

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:34.430
we had a lot of talk about the pandemic,

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.100
we had talks about the
Black Lives Matter movement,

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:40.660
we had talks about being a person

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.492
who may not feel like
they're seen in the space.

00:11:42.492 --> 00:11:46.420
So we really try to connect
you with different things.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.108
We had a career panel this year,

00:11:48.108 --> 00:11:51.010
showing the diversity of faces in science

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:52.300
and the scientific enterprise.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:56.080
We had people who were MDs, PhDs,

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.740
people who are physical therapists.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.423
So we really wanna find ways to connect

00:11:59.423 --> 00:12:03.800
with students outside of the hard science,

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.716
but also build around that
experience and have fun.

00:12:08.716 --> 00:12:11.860
I feel like we tend to forget that.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.570
We think that it's school
and it's something to build

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.250
on our thinking and our knowledge

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:20.160
and we tend to leave fun up.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.830
But RockEDU tries really
hard to incorporate fun

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:24.100
in everything we do.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.930
We actually have an
impromptu group that comes in

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:30.160
and we do these acting things over Zoom,

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:31.770
which at first sounded weird,

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.893
but it was actually an amazing experience.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.480
And so what do these
research tracks look like?

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.940
And so these research tracks are tailored

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:43.060
to what the scientists
are gonna bring forward.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:45.180
So our scientists are actually
gonna start developing

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.890
these research tracks in December.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.420
And so by June, everything is
put together and fine tuned,

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.900
and we're able to send
you all the science kits

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:55.870
to each students.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.950
We survey all of our students to ensure

00:12:57.950 --> 00:12:59.830
that whatever kits we're sending

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:02.010
is suitable for the
environment that you're in.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.550
So we wanna make sure that everyone

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:05.840
feels like they're included.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.690
We wanna make sure that everyone
has an equal opportunity

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:10.340
to do what they're interested in.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.923
And so these materials
will be mailed to you.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:19.620
I believe last year, we did FedEx,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.689
you'll get it in a few days.

00:13:21.689 --> 00:13:24.825
You don't need any prior
research experience.

00:13:24.825 --> 00:13:27.660
This is something that
you come in open-minded

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.584
just ready to dive right in.

00:13:29.584 --> 00:13:33.740
And I think what I really
love about this method

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.470
of doing science is that
you have the ability

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:39.950
to put your own design and
your own knowledge to it.

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:42.710
So instead of being in a
laboratory, where you're taking,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:44.980
don't wanna say orders
where you're taking orders

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:48.640
from the teacher and the laboratory,

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.237
you're kind of just taking this kit

00:13:50.237 --> 00:13:55.237
and molding it into what you
feel that it should grow into.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:56.950
And so you have a lot of autonomy

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:58.550
over the work that you're doing,

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.123
and you have mentors
that are rooting for you,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.010
and a diverse amount of trainees

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.160
that are here to help you out.

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:09.780
So some of the tracks
that we had last year

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.860
and some of the themes
that surrounded that,

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.090
we had our RNA Bioinformatics,

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.780
and our Protein Structure and
Alzheimer's research track,

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:18.998
and those were more so computer-based

00:14:18.998 --> 00:14:21.260
and like programming-based.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.967
We also had a microbiology.

00:14:22.967 --> 00:14:25.250
We had two tracks under microbiology.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.270
So we had a yeast and bacteria track

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.577
where students were able to cultivate

00:14:28.577 --> 00:14:31.580
these microbes safely in their homes.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.930
And we also had an ant
social biology track

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.730
where students had the option

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.510
of observing ants in the
wild or capturing ants

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:41.983
and developing experimental
studies on that.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:47.320
So what do students get out of it?

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:48.770
What do you get out of it?

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.610
And so if you're not convinced

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:52.150
that this might be the program for you,

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.350
for the students that are listening in,

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.260
this is, once again, I'm gonna reiterate

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.410
that it is college and career support.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:02.190
So at the end, we see that
you've put in the best effort,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.910
you seem super passionate
from the beginning to the end,

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:07.570
you're definitely more than happy,

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:11.320
and we beg you to access the
letters of recommendation

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.010
that can be from the RockEDU team

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:14.990
or that can be from your research mentors,

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:17.770
or it can be from the
facilitators from the program.

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.840
Each one of you or each
one of the students

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:23.430
that get into the program
will be assigned an advocate.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:28.300
And this is like you have
your own science person

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:29.810
that you can connect with.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:33.120
And so it's kind of like
having a scientist at hand

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.160
where you can call if
you have any questions

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:36.201
outside of your project

00:15:36.201 --> 00:15:38.970
if you need help with
anything that you're doing,

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.694
and if they don't know how to help you,

00:15:40.694 --> 00:15:44.910
we can find means and ways
to provide that help for you.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.210
You're gonna be a group
with other students

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.210
who are like-minded as yourself.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:52.010
So all these science nerds coming together

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.626
to have a really great time.

00:15:53.626 --> 00:15:56.200
We take in 16 students and we try our best

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:00.010
to make our virtual LAB Jumpstart
as engaging as possible.

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:01.945
So you're not only engaging
with the scientists

00:16:01.945 --> 00:16:03.991
and the facilitators and the mentors,

00:16:03.991 --> 00:16:05.780
but you're also engaging with the students

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:07.913
that are involved in this process.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.934
More importantly, this
is laying the foundation

00:16:10.934 --> 00:16:12.790
for your network in STEM.

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:13.867
You're gonna meet amazing students,

00:16:13.867 --> 00:16:16.230
you're gonna meet amazing scientists,

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:17.980
you're gonna meet amazing mentors,

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:21.320
and so they're gonna
definitely help build.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:23.330
For all of our LAB Jumpstart students,

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:26.160
we help you build your LinkedIn website.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.880
So it's like the start of
your professional world.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:33.660
And even more importantly, you
get paid for your commitment.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:36.170
So we know this is a
larger commitment time,

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:37.820
but it's definitely worth it.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:41.890
But we're also paying 2,250 stipend

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.870
that will cover the spring and the summer,

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:45.970
just to reflect the amount of
time that you've committed.

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.250
We don't want any
students to have to choose

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:52.280
between having a summer job
or being involved in something

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:54.460
that they're passionate
about, such as science.

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:56.665
So really finding ways to
break down those barriers,

00:16:56.665 --> 00:17:00.423
and we're hoping that a stipend
can help alleviate that.

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:06.090
So if you're interested
and you want to apply,

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:08.440
there are some important
dates to keep in mind.

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:13.050
So October 5th, so a couple of weeks ago,

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:14.240
nominations are open,

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:17.840
so you do need to be
nominated by an adult.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:19.257
Once you're nominated by an adult,

00:17:19.257 --> 00:17:21.500
the application would open for you

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.830
and you can begin your application.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:27.060
Nominations end on December 21st.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.264
And so after nominations close,

00:17:30.264 --> 00:17:33.840
you won't be able to get
someone to nominate you,

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.680
but the applications would
close on January 4th.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.680
So even on the last day
of you getting nominated,

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:42.080
you have a few weeks to
get your application in.

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.513
What does application process look like?

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:53.466
So step one, like I mentioned,
if you're interested,

00:17:53.466 --> 00:17:55.691
find someone to nominate you.

00:17:55.691 --> 00:17:57.240
Once you're nominated,

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.440
you're gonna get a link
to start your application,

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.383
and that would be sent to
the email that you gave.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:06.630
Once you get that application

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.560
and you finish your application,

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:11.320
it's submitted, it's
reviewed by our staff,

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:13.910
grad students, facilitators.

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.020
And out of that group of applications,

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:18.380
we bring in about 20, well, this time,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.764
we're bringing you in through Zoom,

00:18:21.764 --> 00:18:26.400
and about 20 students will
be interviewed virtually.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.140
From the batch of about 20 students,

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.230
we'll pick 16 to move forward

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:31.953
through the LAB Jumpstart program.

00:18:33.846 --> 00:18:36.620
Once you finish your
interview, by February,

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:39.520
you'll be notified on your status.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:42.290
And so you'll get an email indicating

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:46.530
whether or not you got in or
if to apply again another time.

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:48.760
We'll also give you other opportunities

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.760
that you can apply to if you
did not get into LAB Jumpstart.

00:18:53.382 --> 00:18:54.898
There are some several components involved

00:18:54.898 --> 00:18:56.700
in the application.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.870
You will need to submit
a personal statement.

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:04.010
You also need to provide a
commentary on a scientific study.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:07.270
We'll provide the article
for you in the application.

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:09.330
You also need to provide a resume

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.770
and an unofficial transcript

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.023
and two letters of recommendations.

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:19.950
And so who's eligible?

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:24.440
So once again, you just have
to be nominated by an adult

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:25.800
and this can be anyone.

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:29.990
It can be your teacher, (coughs) sorry,

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:32.150
it could be a teacher, your mentor,

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:35.300
your pastor, your choir director,

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:39.090
someone who can attest to
who you are as a person,

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.303
your passion for the sciences.

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:47.100
You must be aged 16 years or older

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:50.370
by the start of LAB Jumpstart,
so that's March 2nd.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:53.080
There are no exceptions to that rule.

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:55.540
You must attend a school in New York City

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:57.240
and you must commit to the entirety

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:01.020
of the program and that's
from March 2nd to August 12th.

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:02.150
And on the right here,

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.770
you just have some of our
past LAB Jumpstart students

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:06.770
from the 2020 cohort.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.300
So do we did a Flipgrid assignment

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:12.320
where we were able to talk about

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:14.390
what LAB Jumpstart meant to everyone.

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:18.110
And as you can see, they
look like they had a blast,

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.033
as I'm sure that they did.

