00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:06.770
that could possibly help us?

00:40:06.770 --> 00:40:10.360
Yeah, so for the Jumpstart program,

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:13.790
like I mentioned, we have our advocates.

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:18.790
And so our Jumpstart advocates
are there to do just that.

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:22.105
And so we're hoping that
they will be the ones

00:40:22.105 --> 00:40:25.160
who can help you with
your college applications.

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:27.130
And so you'll be given
their email addresses,

00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:28.596
they'll be checking in on you,

00:40:28.596 --> 00:40:32.340
find out where you are in the
college application process,

00:40:32.340 --> 00:40:35.000
if they can find someone else
who may be able to help you,

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:37.514
if we can find someone, we're
more than happy to do so.

00:40:37.514 --> 00:40:40.280
We wanna make sure that all
of our Jumpstart scholars

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:43.264
are successful in that
college application process

00:40:43.264 --> 00:40:46.157
and also successful in college.

00:40:46.157 --> 00:40:50.795
We always ask our Jumpstart
scholars to stay in touch

00:40:50.795 --> 00:40:53.451
so we can help in terms of networking

00:40:53.451 --> 00:40:56.723
and getting you the people that you need

00:40:56.723 --> 00:40:58.763
to help you wherever you are.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:04.923
Do we work in teams or as a class? Both.

00:41:06.299 --> 00:41:07.875
So there may be some sessions

00:41:07.875 --> 00:41:10.480
where we're working
together in a large group.

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:15.480
So say on a Tuesday, we'll be
talking about the background

00:41:15.824 --> 00:41:17.789
or giving you some type of knowledge,

00:41:17.789 --> 00:41:20.530
will be like interactive lecture,

00:41:20.530 --> 00:41:23.040
and then we might split
you off into breakout rooms

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:24.585
to work together as a team.

00:41:24.585 --> 00:41:29.143
You definitely have to work in
teams, you can't bypass that.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:33.753
So even if you feel like you
don't like to work in teams,

00:41:33.753 --> 00:41:37.650
it will be a thing that you'll
learn during this process,

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:41.930
which will be very helpful
outside of LAB Jumpstart

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:43.263
and the summer program.

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:48.240
So we'll be doing both team
and class, team and class.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:49.860
For the list of laboratories,

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:51.770
only four of them will be picked

00:41:51.770 --> 00:41:54.373
to be the theme of research tracks?

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:03.350
We're not picking like a specific four,

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:06.846
we're kinda developing
based on the expertise

00:42:06.846 --> 00:42:09.627
of the labs that we have.

00:42:09.627 --> 00:42:11.470
I don't know if Jeanne
wants to add to that.

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:13.670
- [Jeanne] Yeah, so
basically, how it works

00:42:13.670 --> 00:42:17.560
is we we develop a pool of mentors

00:42:17.560 --> 00:42:19.970
that want to participate on research team.

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:21.390
And each of those people,

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:23.006
even though they're coming from a variety,

00:42:23.006 --> 00:42:25.560
they're coming from a
variety of different labs,

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:28.380
we might have a lab,
we might have a postdoc

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:32.580
who studies skin cancer,

00:42:32.580 --> 00:42:36.700
we might have a postdoc
who studies DNA evolution,

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:37.750
something like that, right?

00:42:37.750 --> 00:42:39.130
So everybody's coming in

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:41.920
with having studied different topics.

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:44.966
But what we do is we figure
out what types of skills

00:42:44.966 --> 00:42:47.340
they might have that overlap.

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:49.730
So for example, we might
have a group of scientists

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:54.197
who are really good at doing
modeling and bioinformatics

00:42:54.197 --> 00:42:55.700
in a variety of different ways.

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:57.440
So we might put them together

00:42:57.440 --> 00:43:00.130
to create a bioinformatics track, right?

00:43:00.130 --> 00:43:03.300
Or for example, in the yeast track,

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.693
Odaelys and I both have a
background in microbiology,

00:43:05.693 --> 00:43:09.110
and we were the main facilitators
for the Wild Yeast Track.

00:43:09.110 --> 00:43:13.160
And we brought in experts to
help out with image analysis.

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:14.910
We brought in experts to help out

00:43:14.910 --> 00:43:18.546
with just general yeast culture,

00:43:18.546 --> 00:43:23.546
and also some modeling using
our using R studio, right?

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:29.570
So the projects emerge based
on the collective skill sets

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:33.890
of the scientists who are coming in

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:35.800
to participate as mentors.

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:38.528
And the reason why we're
not able to tell you

00:43:38.528 --> 00:43:43.393
about that right now is
because the mentors are not,

00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:50.320
we don't get them together
until March or so,

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:51.780
'cause we have to give scientists time

00:43:51.780 --> 00:43:53.130
to think about their projects

00:43:53.130 --> 00:43:55.523
and also determine their availabilities.

00:43:56.438 --> 00:44:01.438
So, those topics will
certainly be more developed

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:06.481
and transparent to the Jumpstart students

00:44:06.481 --> 00:44:09.290
sometime in middle to late spring.

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:11.990
I hope that's an adequate
answer for your question,

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:13.140
and thank you for this.

00:44:15.412 --> 00:44:20.412
- There was another question,
did we answered it already?

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:28.220
So for this, so we're not
looking to give homework.

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:30.840
We're hoping that whatever we give you all

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:33.350
would be that four hours a week.

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:36.480
So whether that's two
hours on Tuesday over Zoom

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.483
and two hours on Tuesday over,

00:44:39.470 --> 00:44:42.460
two hours on Tuesdays and two
hours on Thursdays over Zoom

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:45.206
or an hour over Zoom
and an hour assignment,

00:44:45.206 --> 00:44:47.870
an hour or half an hour in Zoom,

00:44:47.870 --> 00:44:49.510
an hour and a half assignment,

00:44:49.510 --> 00:44:51.560
we're hoping that the
amount of participation,

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:53.910
amount of hours that
you need to participate

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:58.430
will be that four hours a
week, so we're not giving,

00:44:58.430 --> 00:44:59.810
we're not meeting for four hours,

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:03.400
and then telling you to go
home and do an extra four hours

00:45:03.400 --> 00:45:04.990
in preparation to come back next week

00:45:04.990 --> 00:45:08.053
and do another like eight
hours, if that makes sense.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:11.270
If there's any types of homework

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:13.270
or be like more so your enrichment,

00:45:13.270 --> 00:45:16.270
so maybe it wasn't something
that was quite clear,

00:45:16.270 --> 00:45:19.300
and you wanted to go back
over to Zoom recording,

00:45:19.300 --> 00:45:21.493
or you wanted to reach
out about a clarification,

00:45:21.493 --> 00:45:24.320
or you wanted to do some
extra reading on your own,

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:27.120
that's fine, but we're not necessarily...

00:45:27.120 --> 00:45:29.230
it's not, we're staying away

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:31.540
from it being a classroom, right?

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:35.860
So this whole program is not us lecturing

00:45:35.860 --> 00:45:39.050
to you over Zoom, it's
gonna be discussion-based,

00:45:39.050 --> 00:45:41.830
it's gonna be engaging,
it's gonna be hands on,

00:45:41.830 --> 00:45:45.548
it's gonna be talking
about science as a whole.

00:45:45.548 --> 00:45:47.523
And this is also going alongside,

00:45:47.523 --> 00:45:50.400
like what Jeanne was mentioning is that,

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:53.855
I wouldn't put too much focus
on the projects themselves

00:45:53.855 --> 00:45:56.297
because one misconception is that,

00:45:56.297 --> 00:46:00.510
"Oh, I'm interested in bacteria, right?

00:46:00.510 --> 00:46:02.210
And that's the only
thing I'm interested in.

00:46:02.210 --> 00:46:04.790
And if I don't get that bacterial project,

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:06.360
I'm not taking part."

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:11.360
When in actuality, I might do
a project on muscles, right?

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:14.898
And a lot of the skills that I'm doing

00:46:14.898 --> 00:46:16.990
with this project on muscles,

00:46:16.990 --> 00:46:19.240
I can transfer to the
skills that I would need

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:21.230
to work on that project with bacteria.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:23.540
So we're really looking at
more of like the process

00:46:23.540 --> 00:46:26.280
that it takes to do a science project

00:46:27.570 --> 00:46:29.078
in terms of critical thinking,

00:46:29.078 --> 00:46:34.078
in terms of thinking about a
problem, analyzing a problem,

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:37.250
what techniques, what skills that you need

00:46:37.250 --> 00:46:39.970
to be successful in that,

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:44.970
how do you navigate through
failing in this experiment?

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:49.500
How do I navigate analyzing
a specific data set, right?

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:54.240
Like Jeanne and Disan, or
whoever else was in the lab,

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:56.410
we may have all done
three different things,

00:46:56.410 --> 00:46:57.800
but we came together to realize

00:46:57.800 --> 00:47:01.560
that we have many of the same tool kits

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:03.110
and many of the same skill sets

00:47:03.110 --> 00:47:06.160
that allowed us to transfer those skills

00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:07.830
towards something completely different

00:47:07.830 --> 00:47:10.030
than what we had done in the laboratory.

00:47:10.030 --> 00:47:11.890
So I really want students to focus in

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:15.920
on we're not just teaching
you about one specific thing,

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:17.994
we're teaching you how to
be successful scientists,

00:47:17.994 --> 00:47:20.850
successful thinkers in the future.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:23.900
And that would definitely come into play

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:27.510
when outside of school,
when you go to college,

00:47:27.510 --> 00:47:29.833
and if you decide to pursue a STEM career.

00:47:31.860 --> 00:47:33.410
So don't be too bogged down

00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:35.958
on like what you think
the topic is gonna be.

00:47:35.958 --> 00:47:37.700
You're gonna get the information

00:47:38.972 --> 00:47:41.790
and we're gonna make it as engaging

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:45.723
and interesting as we can given the Zoom.

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:52.400
I would say that in terms
of large time commitment,

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:53.750
I feel like it is a commitment.

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:56.939
So if you have other things
happening outside of school,

00:47:56.939 --> 00:48:00.350
you might have to pick and
choose what you wanna do.

00:48:00.350 --> 00:48:03.720
So one thing I ask students
is to make sure like,

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:05.670
I understand that you
might have extracurricular,

00:48:05.670 --> 00:48:07.269
but we also need to make sure

00:48:07.269 --> 00:48:10.120
that you will fully
participate in the program.

00:48:10.120 --> 00:48:11.647
So if you're doing like
soccer after school,

00:48:11.647 --> 00:48:15.500
you're also in a music program,
and you're tutoring, right?

00:48:15.500 --> 00:48:19.740
You may have to consider if you can handle

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:21.340
doing all four things

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:23.080
or you may have to put
something to the side

00:48:23.080 --> 00:48:26.105
for one semester and
take on this opportunity

00:48:26.105 --> 00:48:28.990
and then go back to it the next semester.

00:48:28.990 --> 00:48:31.760
So I definitely don't
recommend trying to juggle

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:35.286
more than, I would say,
two extracurricular,

00:48:35.286 --> 00:48:37.440
if you're gonna do Jumpstart.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:41.260
With that, you do need
to be present and there

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:45.270
for this program in
order to be successful.

00:48:45.270 --> 00:48:48.993
We have taken Catholic
school students in Jumpstart,

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:52.523
so feel free to apply as well.

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:57.100
So we don't give college
credits for this program.

00:48:57.100 --> 00:48:59.750
What I think what's even
better than college credits

00:48:59.750 --> 00:49:03.101
is the letter of recommendation
that you're gonna get.

00:49:03.101 --> 00:49:05.043
So I've actually,

00:49:05.043 --> 00:49:06.240
I don't know if this
means anything to anyone,

00:49:06.240 --> 00:49:09.537
but we've had former
students from both Jumpstart

00:49:09.537 --> 00:49:14.240
and SSRP that are going
into science careers

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:17.360
in terms of like becoming
a physician assistant.

00:49:17.360 --> 00:49:20.820
Some are going to
outreach, some are in labs.

00:49:20.820 --> 00:49:24.040
We have students going into Yale, Harvard,

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:25.500
a lot of them have stayed local

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:27.496
and going to universities here.

00:49:27.496 --> 00:49:31.030
In terms of like the SSRP,
the summer program itself,

00:49:31.030 --> 00:49:32.800
I've actually had physicians who told me

00:49:32.800 --> 00:49:34.749
that they were part of the summer program.

00:49:34.749 --> 00:49:37.083
So even though you're not
getting that summer credit,

00:49:37.083 --> 00:49:39.577
I think you're getting
invaluable experiences

00:49:39.577 --> 00:49:42.810
through the Jumpstart program

00:49:42.810 --> 00:49:46.183
that you can take with you beyond college.

00:49:48.810 --> 00:49:51.940
No, you have to commit to
the spring and the summer.

00:49:51.940 --> 00:49:54.373
So for students who can't
commit to the summer,

00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:57.550
we don't recommend that
you apply to the program.

00:49:57.550 --> 00:49:59.693
We do need to full commitment for both,

00:50:00.601 --> 00:50:02.953
the March through August,

00:50:05.111 --> 00:50:07.111
so it's not something that we can split.

00:50:10.570 --> 00:50:11.403
Thank you.

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:18.370
Yeah, it's more of a research opportunity,

00:50:18.370 --> 00:50:19.910
yeah, with other added benefits,

00:50:19.910 --> 00:50:23.250
but you're also gonna learn things

00:50:23.250 --> 00:50:24.850
that would aid in your academics.

00:50:24.850 --> 00:50:27.840
So I've also had Jumpstart students who,

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:29.640
we cover a specific topic,

00:50:29.640 --> 00:50:31.510
and they return a couple weeks after

00:50:31.510 --> 00:50:34.140
and said that the teacher just
went over something similar.

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:36.670
And they were able to quickly catch on

00:50:36.670 --> 00:50:37.776
to what their teacher was saying

00:50:37.776 --> 00:50:40.010
because they were given

00:50:40.010 --> 00:50:42.260
an overview of it here at Jumpstart.

00:50:42.260 --> 00:50:43.722
So even though it is a
research opportunity,

00:50:43.722 --> 00:50:45.670
it does help in your academic,

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:47.390
but I would say it's more so research

00:50:47.390 --> 00:50:49.590
with other things that can aid

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:51.703
in your professional development.

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:58.533
Can you apply to both Jumpstart
and the summer program?

00:51:00.380 --> 00:51:03.247
Yeah, you can.

00:51:05.665 --> 00:51:07.970
Do you think showing that we were a part

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:09.743
of this program might boost?

00:51:11.130 --> 00:51:12.913
Oh, yeah, definitely.

00:51:13.820 --> 00:51:15.740
So getting into Ivy
Leagues, like I mentioned,

00:51:15.740 --> 00:51:17.945
we have several students that have gone

00:51:17.945 --> 00:51:21.286
into Ivy League school after participating

00:51:21.286 --> 00:51:24.130
in Jumpstart and SSRP.

00:51:24.130 --> 00:51:26.814
I think RockEDU is a well-known program

00:51:26.814 --> 00:51:30.860
in terms of student
programming that we have here.

00:51:30.860 --> 00:51:32.080
And like I mentioned,

00:51:32.080 --> 00:51:33.840
the name and the letters of recommendation

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:35.760
can take you a very long way.

00:51:35.760 --> 00:51:38.170
And this is also based on your commitment.

00:51:38.170 --> 00:51:39.417
Like you can't say,

00:51:39.417 --> 00:51:41.070
"Hey, I wanna be a part of this program

00:51:41.070 --> 00:51:42.743
because I know it will
help me in the future."

00:51:42.743 --> 00:51:44.037
Your letter of recommendation

00:51:44.037 --> 00:51:46.620
is also based on what you put in, right?

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:50.330
You can't expect your
mentors and your facilitators

00:51:50.330 --> 00:51:51.610
to write you a great recommendation

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:54.290
if you did not put in
the effort and the work

00:51:54.290 --> 00:51:57.653
that they felt that you should have had.

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:01.800
- [Jeanne] Yeah, and if I
can add to that, Odaelys,

00:52:01.800 --> 00:52:06.800
I'll just add some data to
support you in sharing that.

00:52:08.050 --> 00:52:12.630
We have had students from
LAB Jumpstart attend Harvard,

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:16.908
MIT, Brandeis-

00:52:16.908 --> 00:52:17.831
- Barnard.

00:52:17.831 --> 00:52:18.942
- [Jeanne] Barnard.

00:52:18.942 --> 00:52:20.480
- Full scholarships.

00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:24.003
- [Jeanne] What's the one in
Chicago? University of Chicago.

00:52:25.410 --> 00:52:28.373
Oh, Yale, we've had a couple of Yales.

00:52:28.373 --> 00:52:33.373
We've also had really
awesome Macaulay Honors,

00:52:34.650 --> 00:52:37.760
Hunter College, a couple
of our CUNY schools,

00:52:37.760 --> 00:52:39.474
which are fantastic city college.

00:52:39.474 --> 00:52:40.999
- BU.

00:52:40.999 --> 00:52:45.543
- [Jeanne] BU, yeah, our
students are definitely,

00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:48.665
it's correlation and
causation, this is something.

00:52:48.665 --> 00:52:50.630
Oh, yeah, we've had Columbia.
- Columbia, yep.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:54.680
- [Jeanne] Absolutely, so
correlation and causation

00:52:54.680 --> 00:52:57.240
is something that we'll
go very deeply into

00:52:57.240 --> 00:53:00.560
on our discussions, but we
are certainly correlated

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:02.940
with very, very well-known

00:53:02.940 --> 00:53:05.823
and highly respected universities so.

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:15.680
- Yeah, so can we mention...

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:17.951
Does this program itself
offer any scholarships?

00:53:17.951 --> 00:53:22.254
The scholarship that we're
offering is the stipend.

00:53:22.254 --> 00:53:25.583
I don't think that answered the question.

00:53:25.583 --> 00:53:28.430
Let's see, if we choose to
follow a different career path

00:53:28.430 --> 00:53:33.430
in the future than science,
can we still do the program?

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:36.080
Well, we're hoping to have students

00:53:36.080 --> 00:53:39.010
that are interested in doing science.

00:53:39.010 --> 00:53:40.930
So if you come into the program saying

00:53:40.930 --> 00:53:43.057
that this is something
that you're interested in,

00:53:43.057 --> 00:53:44.763
"I really enjoy STEM.

00:53:45.700 --> 00:53:48.310
I'm just not sure if it's right for me.

00:53:48.310 --> 00:53:51.250
I haven't had the opportunity
to get my feet wet."

00:53:51.250 --> 00:53:52.280
We want you to apply.

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:56.040
If you know from the get go
that you don't even enjoy

00:53:56.040 --> 00:53:57.360
sitting in your science class,

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.827
I probably won't apply to LAB Jumpstart.

00:54:05.454 --> 00:54:08.900
So Jumpstart program and the SSRP program

00:54:08.900 --> 00:54:11.010
are two different programs.

00:54:11.010 --> 00:54:14.640
Jumpstart students are
automatically entered

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:17.740
into the summer program,
if that makes sense.

00:54:17.740 --> 00:54:19.761
So that's why there's
two application process.

00:54:19.761 --> 00:54:22.320
There are two different types
of student groups as well,

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:24.190
like I mentioned, the Jumpstart program

00:54:24.190 --> 00:54:27.090
are for students who were
not afforded the opportunity

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:28.480
to get involved into research.

00:54:28.480 --> 00:54:31.300
So they're usually students
from New York City DOE schools,

00:54:31.300 --> 00:54:33.350
students from underserved communities.

00:54:33.350 --> 00:54:37.230
So for example, we've had
like first gen students.

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:40.050
We've had students who may have gone

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:43.900
to a Catholic school who
just moved to the US.

00:54:43.900 --> 00:54:48.540
We've had students who, I don't know,

00:54:48.540 --> 00:54:50.123
what are some other examples?

00:54:53.500 --> 00:54:55.480
They're all students who are part

00:54:55.480 --> 00:54:59.410
of the New York City School System

00:54:59.410 --> 00:55:01.450
that were not giving opportunities

00:55:01.450 --> 00:55:02.930
that other students may have had,

00:55:02.930 --> 00:55:05.170
and those students usually apply to LAB,

00:55:05.170 --> 00:55:08.763
I mean, to Summer Science
Research Program or SSRP.

00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:14.250
So Rockefeller University

00:55:14.250 --> 00:55:16.950
does not have an
undergraduate institution,

00:55:16.950 --> 00:55:18.890
it's a graduate school.

00:55:18.890 --> 00:55:20.960
I also wanna throw it out there,
The Rockefeller University

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:25.183
is one of the best research
institution in the US.

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:29.285
So that alone puts a lot of weight

00:55:29.285 --> 00:55:32.280
on your participation in the program.

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:34.780
Like I mentioned,
students who are applying

00:55:34.780 --> 00:55:36.610
to these programs should be students

00:55:36.610 --> 00:55:39.490
who are genuinely
interested in doing science.

00:55:39.490 --> 00:55:43.700
If you know that you want
to go into accounting

00:55:43.700 --> 00:55:47.313
or I don't know, what's
something not science-related?

00:55:48.180 --> 00:55:49.810
Something that's not even close to-

00:55:49.810 --> 00:55:51.655
- Theater.
- Theater, right?

00:55:51.655 --> 00:55:55.120
If you do both, like you can
do arts and science, right?

00:55:55.120 --> 00:55:56.840
If you're interested in theater,

00:55:56.840 --> 00:55:58.552
which we had another student, Jacob,

00:55:58.552 --> 00:56:02.540
who would go to theater
classes after Jumpstart,

00:56:02.540 --> 00:56:07.540
or come to Jumpstart after
theater classes, right?

00:56:08.530 --> 00:56:10.500
And now he's doing both in college.

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:11.999
If you're interested in both, apply.

00:56:11.999 --> 00:56:14.100
If you're interested in theater

00:56:14.100 --> 00:56:17.053
and not at all interested
in science, do not apply.

00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:23.119
Yeah, so Jumpstart is only for NYC,

00:56:23.119 --> 00:56:24.881
NYC, including all five boroughs,

00:56:24.881 --> 00:56:29.881
Staten Island, Brooklyn,
Bronx, Manhattan, and Queens.

00:56:31.670 --> 00:56:34.550
And I think the benefit
now of doing it virtually

00:56:34.550 --> 00:56:35.710
is that you don't have to commute.

00:56:35.710 --> 00:56:37.725
We've had students
commuting from Staten Island

00:56:37.725 --> 00:56:40.790
every Tuesday and Thursday after school.

00:56:40.790 --> 00:56:42.840
And so it just shows that how committed

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:44.494
these students were to participating.

00:56:44.494 --> 00:56:49.290
I have students coming
from Red Hook and Brooklyn

00:56:49.290 --> 00:56:53.120
coming to Jumpstart after school,

00:56:53.120 --> 00:56:55.440
students who have to
commute to Coney Island.

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:57.712
And so I think it's really nice

00:56:57.712 --> 00:57:00.360
that you won't have to
travel an hour and a half

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:02.600
to two hours to get here.

00:57:02.600 --> 00:57:04.791
So it definitely opens up the opportunity

00:57:04.791 --> 00:57:06.620
to be involved in something

00:57:06.620 --> 00:57:08.100
that you don't have to travel all the way

00:57:08.100 --> 00:57:10.203
to the Upper East Side to participate in.

00:57:14.350 --> 00:57:16.630
Yeah, so if you know, like I mentioned,

00:57:16.630 --> 00:57:19.450
if you know you're in a school

00:57:19.450 --> 00:57:22.730
that has like an A1 science program,

00:57:22.730 --> 00:57:26.690
you have a lab in your school,
you've done PCR already,

00:57:26.690 --> 00:57:29.600
you've may have worked
in a lab in the summer,

00:57:29.600 --> 00:57:31.710
you have a connection to something else,

00:57:31.710 --> 00:57:34.760
you've done science fairs like 20 times,

00:57:34.760 --> 00:57:36.030
and you've won first place,

00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:37.599
and you've gone on to the nationals,

00:57:37.599 --> 00:57:40.930
Jumpstart is probably
not the program for you.

00:57:40.930 --> 00:57:42.809
You'll definitely be a
competitive applicant

00:57:42.809 --> 00:57:45.370
for the summer program,

00:57:45.370 --> 00:57:47.470
so I'd apply to the Summer
Science Research Program.

00:57:47.470 --> 00:57:50.240
We're really looking for
students in Jumpstart

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:53.333
who we can give the extra support

00:57:53.333 --> 00:57:57.510
and the supplies that we
think would allow them

00:57:57.510 --> 00:57:59.940
to be successful in
the competitive program

00:57:59.940 --> 00:58:00.840
during the summer.

00:58:06.580 --> 00:58:08.913
Is all the work hands on?

00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:12.491
When I say...

00:58:12.491 --> 00:58:14.913
(indistinct) to apply our knowledge.

00:58:16.210 --> 00:58:18.893
- [Jeanne] I just put up an
answer in the chat, yeah.

00:58:19.850 --> 00:58:24.350
Yeah, we really want there
to be an appreciation

00:58:24.350 --> 00:58:29.110
for science as an
element of everyday life.

00:58:29.110 --> 00:58:33.383
But it's also important that
we do get into some theory,

00:58:34.270 --> 00:58:39.010
but you will likely be more the hands on

00:58:39.010 --> 00:58:40.963
than the purely theoretical.

00:58:45.030 --> 00:58:46.830
- It depends on your involvement is.

00:58:46.830 --> 00:58:49.923
If you worked like in a
summer program before,

00:58:51.346 --> 00:58:53.081
like for an extended period of time,

00:58:53.081 --> 00:58:55.607
then I would apply for the summer program.

00:58:55.607 --> 00:58:57.960
If you like worked in a lab

00:58:57.960 --> 00:59:00.593
like a lab as part of your class,

00:59:02.192 --> 00:59:05.640
and you don't think that you
really got the opportunity

00:59:05.640 --> 00:59:09.683
to dive in to science, then I
would say apply to Jumpstart.

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:16.560
- [Jeanne] This is such a
wonderful information session.

00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:18.930
I'm so pumped that there's
all these awesome questions.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:20.397
I'm sorry my video's not on, Odaelys,

00:59:20.397 --> 00:59:22.680
'cause you didn't give me any permissions

00:59:22.680 --> 00:59:24.060
to have my video on but-

00:59:24.060 --> 00:59:25.160
- Oh, I didn't know I had to do it.

00:59:25.998 --> 00:59:28.833
- [Lizzie] That was me, I think. Sorry.

00:59:28.833 --> 00:59:31.183
- [Jeanne] I just wanted
to apologize for that.

00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:36.257
Anyway, yeah. Is there
any other questions?

00:59:36.257 --> 00:59:39.490
Does anybody have any other
questions? This is so exciting.

00:59:39.490 --> 00:59:41.020
I hope that we get a ton of applications

00:59:41.020 --> 00:59:44.870
and that we read all about you all,

00:59:44.870 --> 00:59:46.690
both through the personal statement

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:50.500
and learn about your scientific interests,

00:59:50.500 --> 00:59:52.833
and this is exciting.

00:59:55.310 --> 00:59:56.560
- [Doug] I just want to really quickly say

00:59:56.560 --> 00:59:58.580
that 'cause I know we've
gotten a few questions about,

00:59:58.580 --> 01:00:00.162
which program do I apply to?

01:00:00.162 --> 01:00:03.750
Don't freak out about, "Oh, I
applied to the wrong program."

01:00:03.750 --> 01:00:05.710
We have moved students' applications

01:00:05.710 --> 01:00:08.580
from either the Jumpstart path into SSRP

01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:10.440
or from SSRP into Jumpstart

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:11.966
if we think that you
are a better candidate

01:00:11.966 --> 01:00:13.610
for a particular pathway.

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:16.897
So don't overthink it too
much, don't worry too much.

01:00:16.897 --> 01:00:19.230
We'll make sure you
get to the right place.

01:00:19.230 --> 01:00:21.240
And if you have questions
on a case by case basis,

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:23.040
reach out, and we'll try to clarify.

01:00:24.730 --> 01:00:27.520
- Yes, you can definite, we would love

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:29.659
if you can join the program,
apply to the program

01:00:29.659 --> 01:00:32.017
if you love science and have an interest

01:00:32.017 --> 01:00:35.590
and you wanna test it out. (giggles)

01:00:35.590 --> 01:00:37.050
- [Jeanne] Yeah, I will add that.

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:38.617
I think one of the amazing things

01:00:38.617 --> 01:00:41.190
that you can get out of Jumpstart is,

01:00:41.190 --> 01:00:43.600
I mean, obviously, is a pathway

01:00:43.600 --> 01:00:45.160
if you wanna go into science,

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:46.810
but I think just as importantly,

01:00:46.810 --> 01:00:50.410
it gives you an opportunity
in a fairly low stakes way

01:00:50.410 --> 01:00:53.290
relative to the rest of your life,

01:00:53.290 --> 01:00:55.640
discover that maybe
you don't love science,

01:00:55.640 --> 01:00:56.900
and I think that's okay, too.

01:00:56.900 --> 01:00:59.521
So I just wanna push that out there.

01:00:59.521 --> 01:01:02.040
We want you to feel successful,

01:01:02.040 --> 01:01:03.950
that is our ultimate goal,

01:01:03.950 --> 01:01:06.440
whether that means success means for you

01:01:06.440 --> 01:01:08.820
that you start off and
pursue some sort of PhD

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:12.180
down the road, or maybe
success for you means

01:01:12.180 --> 01:01:15.767
that you just had a
good time experimenting,

01:01:15.767 --> 01:01:18.470
but you really wanna go
and be a lawyer, right?

01:01:18.470 --> 01:01:21.343
That's okay, too, so we are here for you,

01:01:22.440 --> 01:01:26.077
and we really wanna elevate
everyone's opportunities

01:01:26.077 --> 01:01:29.373
to participate in an
authentic process of science.

01:01:31.930 --> 01:01:32.900
- I agree.

01:01:32.900 --> 01:01:34.640
- Yeah, so both programs are challenging.

01:01:34.640 --> 01:01:37.860
Like I mentioned, if you feel
like you have all the tools

01:01:37.860 --> 01:01:42.860
and you're a competitive applicant

01:01:43.390 --> 01:01:44.570
and you're a straight A student,

01:01:44.570 --> 01:01:48.640
I would apply to both programs
and see what that fits.

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:50.370
Might be that your application

01:01:50.370 --> 01:01:52.763
can just go straight
through to summer program.

01:01:56.020 --> 01:01:59.580
But yes, they will both
be challenging, I think.

01:01:59.580 --> 01:02:03.520
I haven't had anyone say
that it was easy breezy.

01:02:03.520 --> 01:02:07.960
It was fun, but we we try
our best to go into your,

01:02:07.960 --> 01:02:09.140
I don't wanna say, go into your heads,

01:02:09.140 --> 01:02:10.810
but we wanna try our best

01:02:10.810 --> 01:02:13.490
to get your brain juices going, right?

01:02:13.490 --> 01:02:16.314
Have you thinking about stuff,
thinking outside the box,

01:02:16.314 --> 01:02:20.710
thinking critically,
thinking of science in a way

01:02:20.710 --> 01:02:23.569
that may not have necessarily been the way

01:02:23.569 --> 01:02:26.003
that it was taught in your classes.

01:02:27.100 --> 01:02:28.970
Kind of like giving you a real view

01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:30.730
of what science actually is

01:02:30.730 --> 01:02:34.483
and what it takes to be involved
in that scientific process.

01:02:35.620 --> 01:02:37.530
- Yes, we expect failure.

01:02:37.530 --> 01:02:42.530
And in fact, we thrive and think
it's a necessary component.

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:47.240
So yes, the challenge will be present,

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:51.430
but it also, we will not
let it crush anybody either.

01:02:51.430 --> 01:02:53.593
So that's a good, we're gonna

01:02:53.593 --> 01:02:55.549
find that balance.
- All the support, yeah.

01:02:55.549 --> 01:02:58.247
You have the support, we wanna
make sure we have sessions

01:02:58.247 --> 01:03:00.810
where it's just about having fun, right?

01:03:00.810 --> 01:03:03.783
We do like these impromptu session

01:03:03.783 --> 01:03:05.380
where we have a group come in,

01:03:05.380 --> 01:03:09.090
and we do like acting,
right, through the video.

01:03:09.090 --> 01:03:12.330
We have time where we just
have conversations, right?

01:03:12.330 --> 01:03:13.910
Maybe we take the first 15 minutes

01:03:13.910 --> 01:03:15.670
and talk about like how's school going?

01:03:15.670 --> 01:03:18.230
How's your mental health,
your emotional health?

01:03:18.230 --> 01:03:19.869
Just to debrief, right?

01:03:19.869 --> 01:03:22.083
Leaving with a quote of,

01:03:22.083 --> 01:03:24.215
hey, this is something to feel good about.

01:03:24.215 --> 01:03:26.180
This is something to think about.

01:03:26.180 --> 01:03:28.957
And so we wanna make sure
that we're hitting all parts

01:03:28.957 --> 01:03:33.957
of you as a person, and
doing that through science,

01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:36.470
if that makes sense.

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:39.960
- Yeah, I'm just so like, I'm pumped up

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:42.830
by such an amazing showing,

01:03:42.830 --> 01:03:45.070
and the questions are
non-stop that I just,

01:03:45.070 --> 01:03:48.303
I'm gonna jump on my soapbox
one more time and say,

01:03:49.250 --> 01:03:52.470
this program is designed
to support people.

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:55.540
And then once we have a
good support of people,

01:03:55.540 --> 01:03:57.560
we show them how to do science, right?

01:03:57.560 --> 01:04:02.240
We will never lose sight that
science is a human endeavor.

01:04:02.240 --> 01:04:04.900
We want people to feel
good about themselves,

01:04:04.900 --> 01:04:07.690
feel good about the science and excel,

01:04:07.690 --> 01:04:10.363
be challenged, appreciate failure.

01:04:11.210 --> 01:04:14.930
We are gonna make it pretty
difficult and challenging

01:04:14.930 --> 01:04:16.643
for you at an academic level,

01:04:16.643 --> 01:04:20.450
but we'll make sure that
you're being supported

01:04:20.450 --> 01:04:24.790
in the other ways as best as
we can within our capacity.

01:04:24.790 --> 01:04:28.740
Again, we wanna push you to do
the best you can possibly do,

01:04:28.740 --> 01:04:29.853
and that is the goal.

01:04:31.945 --> 01:04:36.890
- These are great questions.
Thank you all for coming in.

01:04:36.890 --> 01:04:38.140
Thank you for your interest.

01:04:38.140 --> 01:04:41.083
I'm hoping to see the
applications pour in.

01:04:42.909 --> 01:04:46.900
I'm hoping to ponder
over many applications

01:04:46.900 --> 01:04:49.633
to pick the chosen 16.

